==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|flaued}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|flaw|ed}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/flɔːd/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/flɔd/|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/flɑd/|a=cot-caught}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔːd|s=1}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Having a [[flaw]] or [[imperfection]].
#: {{ant|en|perfect|flawless|Thesaurus:flawless}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Flawed''' diamonds are generally not used in jewellery.}}
#: {{ux|en|His design for a [[perpetual motion machine]] is '''flawed''' because water does not flow uphill.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=June 14 2018|author=Timothy Snyder|journal=The New York Times|title=How Did the Nazis Gain Power in Germany?
|passage=He presents Hitler’s rise as an element of the collapse of a republic confronting dilemmas of globalization with imperfect instruments and '''flawed''' leaders.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 March 8|author=David Clough|title=The long road that led to Beeching|journal=RAIL|issue=978|page=42|text=Yet he was correct on every point, meaning that the Plan was deeply '''flawed''' from the start.}}

